#~
One thread closes a plain TCP socket while another is using it

A TCPSocket or TCPSocketServer object holds its descriptor in one word, -1 once it is
closed. Closing a socket from another thread is how a server's accept loop is stopped and
how a blocked read is ended, so that word is read by one thread while another stores -1
in it. The VM read and wrote it as a plain word: ThreadSanitizer reported SockTcpAccept
against SockTcpClose in the v2026.11.4 soak. And Close() read the descriptor, then stored
-1, then closed it, so two threads closing one socket could both read the descriptor and
both close it -- the second close() landing on whatever the process had opened under that
number in between.

The word is now loaded and stored atomically, and Close() takes the descriptor out with
one exchange, so exactly one of two closers closes it.

Three shapes, each many times, with no sleeps and no shared flags of the test's own (so a
ThreadSanitizer run of this file, tools/tsan/run_tsan.sh, has only the VM to report on):

1. a thread in Accept() while Main closes the listener: the thread's Accept() answers Nil
   or a socket that is not open, and the thread ends;
2. two threads close one connected socket at the same moment, while Main opens and closes
   sockets of its own: a descriptor closed twice would close one of those, and its
   write would then fail;
3. one thread half-closes a connected socket (CloseGracefully sends the FIN and waits for
   the peer) while another closes it.

The assertions are that every thread ends, that Main's own sockets keep working, and
the exit status.
~#
use System.Concurrency, System.IO.Net;

# accepts until its listener is closed under it; closes whatever it accepted
class Acceptor from Thread {
	@server : TCPSocketServer;

	New(server : TCPSocketServer) {
		Parent("acceptor");
		@server := server;
	}

	method : public : Run(param : System.Base) ~ Nil {
		while(true) {
			client := @server->Accept();
			if(client = Nil) {
				return;
			};
			if(<>client->IsOpen()) {
				return;
			};
			client->Close();
		};
	}
}

class Closer from Thread {
	@socket : TCPSocket;
	@graceful : Bool;

	New(socket : TCPSocket, graceful : Bool) {
		Parent("closer");
		@socket := socket;
		@graceful := graceful;
	}

	method : public : Run(param : System.Base) ~ Nil {
		if(@graceful) {
			@socket->CloseGracefully(50);
		}
		else {
			@socket->Close();
		};
	}
}

class SocketCloseRaces {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		failures := 0;

		# 1. Close() under Accept()
		for(i := 0; i < 40; i += 1;) {
			server := TCPSocketServer->New(0);
			if(<>server->Listen(4)) {
				"FAIL: listen (accept race {$i})"->PrintLine();
				Runtime->Exit(1);
			};
			acceptor := Acceptor->New(server);
			acceptor->Execute(Nil);
			# every other round a connection first, so the thread is between two Accept() calls
			if(i % 2 = 0) {
				probe := TCPSocket->New("127.0.0.1", server->GetPort());
				probe->Close();
			};
			server->Close();
			acceptor->Join();
		};
		"ok: 40 listeners closed under their accept threads"->PrintLine();

		# 2 and 3. two threads end one connected socket
		listener := TCPSocketServer->New(0);
		if(<>listener->Listen(16)) {
			"FAIL: listen"->PrintLine();
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
		port := listener->GetPort();

		for(i := 0; i < 60; i += 1;) {
			shared := TCPSocket->New("127.0.0.1", port);
			peer := listener->Accept();
			if(<>shared->IsOpen() | peer = Nil) {
				"FAIL: connect (close race {$i})"->PrintLine();
				Runtime->Exit(1);
			};

			first := Closer->New(shared, i % 3 = 2);
			second := Closer->New(shared, false);
			first->Execute(Nil);
			second->Execute(Nil);

			# Main's own connection, opened and used while the two close: a descriptor
			# closed twice is likely to be this one's
			mine := TCPSocket->New("127.0.0.1", port);
			mine_peer := listener->Accept();
			if(<>mine->IsOpen() | mine_peer = Nil) {
				"FAIL: connect (own socket {$i})"->PrintLine();
				Runtime->Exit(1);
			};

			first->Join();
			second->Join();

			mine->WriteString("ping\r\n");
			line := mine_peer->ReadLine();
			if(line = Nil | <>line->Equals("ping")) {
				failures += 1;
			};
			mine->Close();
			mine_peer->Close();
			peer->Close();

			if(shared->IsOpen()) {
				failures += 1;
			};
		};
		listener->Close();

		if(failures > 0) {
			"FAIL: {$failures} round(s) lost a socket of their own or left the shared one open"->PrintLine();
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
		"ok: 60 sockets closed by two threads at once, Main's own sockets unharmed"->PrintLine();
	}
}
